+# Decision: Whole-visit revisions for concurrent visit edits
+
+## Context
+
+Tablets stay offline for up to 48 hours, and workers sometimes correct the same visit independently after comparing field notes. Edits derived from an earlier fetched state therefore arrive days later, after other edits to the same visit have already been accepted. The record store must turn such arrivals into a current record without treating arrival order as authority and without discarding any submission (HLD tenets). Two concurrency mechanisms are in the domain for that turn: whole-visit revisions and field-aware three-way merge. This decision fixes the mechanism tablet-sync and review-export are sized around.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+Weighted criteria, in descending importance:
+
+- **Scientific integrity of the current record — major.** Related fields — a species identification and its cover estimate, an observer list and notes that reference those observers — must not be recombined by rules that cannot see the relationship. Importance derives from the tenet "Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable."
+- **Implementation and maintenance burden — moderate.** Field-aware merging needs a maintained rule set per field shape (scalars, observer lists, species entries) kept current as the visit schema evolves, plus field-level provenance machinery. Whole-visit revisions need revision storage, head tracking, and a comparison workflow, and no merge-rule surface. Importance derives from the tenet "Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator."
+- **Review workload for the two coordinators — moderate.** How much same-visit concurrent edit traffic becomes coordinator work before the weekly export. The team has stated it will accept a larger queue rather than risk automated combination.
+- **Export readiness between reviews — moderate.** How many visits reach the Friday export without coordinator intervention.
+- **Submission and local-state complexity on the tablets — minor.** What tablets must retain and construct per edit beyond the visit content a worker already entered.
+
+No gate eliminates an option in contention: both mechanisms retain every original submission, per the inherited no-discard invariant.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+**Description.** Every accepted edit is a complete revision of the visit — all scientific fields, not a delta. Each submission names the base revision it started from, meaning the head the submitting worker last fetched. A submission whose base is the visit's current sole head extends the chain. A submission whose base is stale is stored as a competing revision; the visit becomes unresolved and stays unresolved until a coordinator resolves the competing heads. The store never designates a current record by arrival order. The current record is always a stored revision: a worker's submission or a coordinator's resolution revision.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Scientific integrity (major): strong — the current record is always a whole submitted or coordinator-resolved visit; no rule ever recombines fields.
+- Implementation and maintenance (moderate): strong — revision storage, head tracking, and a comparison view; no merge-rule surface to maintain.
+- Review workload (moderate): partial — every same-visit concurrent edit, even edits to unrelated fields, becomes coordinator work.
+- Export readiness (moderate): partial — visits with competing heads are held out of the export set until resolved.
+- Tablet complexity (minor): strong — a base revision ID plus the complete payload the tablet already holds; no local differencing.
+
+**Summary.** The simplest mechanism that keeps every current value attributable to a whole submitted visit; pays in coordinator time.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+**Description.** Each submission carries its base snapshot and a structured change list. The server combines edits whose changed fields do not overlap and routes only overlapping changes to review. Maintained rules define combination for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Field-level provenance records which submission supplied each value of the combined record.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Scientific integrity (major): weak — rules that combine correctly by field shape can still cross scientific relationships the rules do not model; revising a species identification and its cover estimate is two fields carrying one scientific intent.
+- Implementation and maintenance (moderate): weak — a rule set per field shape, maintained as the visit schema evolves, plus provenance machinery the review side must render.
+- Review workload (moderate): strong — unrelated concurrent edits need no coordinator time.
+- Export readiness (moderate): strong — most concurrent edits produce an export-ready record without intervention.
+- Tablet complexity (minor): partial — tablets must retain base snapshots and produce structured change lists rather than whole payloads.
+
+**Summary.** Trades implementation burden and scientific-integrity risk for coordinator time.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Whole-visit revisions.**
+
+It wins on the major criterion and on maintenance burden: the current record never contains a value that no one submitted as part of a whole visit, and the store carries no merge-rule surface to keep current as the visit schema evolves. The cost — every same-visit concurrent edit becomes review work even when the edits are unrelated — is accepted deliberately. At this team's scale (two coordinators, a weekly export, occasional same-visit concurrency), a larger review queue is the cheaper error than an unnoticed recombination of related scientific fields. The decision turns on the tenets "Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable" and "Do not silently discard a submitted edit."
+
+**Downstream obligations.** Tablet-sync retains base revision IDs on local drafts and builds its retry state machine around a submission envelope that carries one; it explains competing-revision outcomes to workers from this node's representation rather than by tablet-side inference. Review-export builds a revision comparison workflow for coordinators, treats resolution as this node's operation, and excludes visits with competing heads from the export set. Record-store defines the submission envelope, the head and unresolved representation, and the resolution operation; both siblings link back rather than inventing concurrency semantics of their own.
+
+**Foreclosures, and when to reopen.** Field-level automatic combination is out for the first release, and an exported value can be explained only at revision granularity — which submission or resolution it traces to, not which field of which submission. Reopen the choice if same-visit concurrent edits grow beyond what two coordinators can clear before the weekly export, measured as a sustained backlog, or if the team finds that almost all concurrent edits are in fact disjoint and reviewing them is mechanical. At that point field-aware merge is weighed against the same criteria, not assumed better.
