+The concurrency mechanism is whole-visit revisions: every accepted edit is a complete revision naming the revision it started from, and concurrent edits become competing revisions for a coordinator to resolve. The rationale and the rejected alternative are recorded in [decisions/whole-visit-revisions.md](decisions/whole-visit-revisions.md).
 
-## Architecture
+Tablet-sync and review-export are not yet designed; both are sized around this node's contract.
 
-The record store exposes authenticated submission, current-state retrieval, submission-history retrieval, and review-outcome operations. Persistence keeps original submissions, their authors, and server receipt times. A submission carries an idempotency key so a connection failure does not create another edit on retry.
+## Revision Model
 
-A visit's scientific fields include observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, and notes. Some edits are unrelated, such as correcting an observer name and adding a missing species. Other edits are related, such as revising a species identification and its cover estimate. The server cannot infer all such relationships from field names.
+A visit is a graph of revisions. A revision carries the complete visit record — plot reference, observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, notes — never a delta. A revision may change any field of the visit record. Each revision records:
 
-The selected concurrency policy will define the remaining submission metadata and review operations. They are deliberately not specified yet.
+- the visit ID it belongs to;
+- a server-assigned revision ID;
+- its parent revision ID, absent on a visit's first revision;
+- the author's worker ID and the server receipt time;
+- the idempotency key carried by its submission;
+- whether it is a resolution revision, and if so, the revision IDs it resolved.
 
-## Submission and Retrieval
+A head is a revision that no later revision names as a parent. A visit with exactly one head has a current record: that head. A visit with more than one head is unresolved; its heads are competing revisions, and the store designates none of them as current. Receipt time is metadata, not precedence.
 
-Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolved or combined representation. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
+Stored revisions are never rewritten or deleted. The original payload of every accepted submission is retained for the life of the visit.
 
-Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work. Exactly how those states are represented remains part of the open choice.
+## Submission
 
-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+The submission envelope carries the visit ID, the base revision ID the edit started from, the complete visit payload, and an idempotency key. The authenticated worker is the author. A first submission for a new visit omits the base revision ID; once a visit exists, every edit must name one.
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+The store validates the payload's field types and its plot reference against the site catalog before acceptance. A new visit must reference an active plot; an existing visit may be edited while its plot is archived.
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+Acceptance is serialized per visit. Each accepted submission becomes a new revision whose parent is the named base revision, and the visit's head set is recomputed. A base naming the sole head extends the chain. A base naming an older revision adds a competing revision and leaves the visit unresolved, including when the visit was already unresolved and the base names one of its heads. A base naming a revision of a different visit, or an omitted base on an existing visit, is rejected with no stored submission.
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+A retry carrying the same idempotency key and an identical payload returns the original acceptance result — including whether the submission became a head or a competing revision — without storing another submission. The same key with a different payload is rejected and leaves the original submission unchanged. Keys are scoped to the author and the visit.
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+## Retrieval
 
-## Downstream Contract Work
+Retrieving a visit returns its head revisions in full — payload, author, receipt time, revision ID, parent — and an indicator of whether the visit is unresolved. A single-head visit presents one current record; an unresolved visit presents every competing revision, and the caller must not treat any of them as preferred.
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+Submission-history retrieval returns every revision of the visit, heads and superseded alike, with the same metadata plus each revision's parent, resolution flag, and resolved revision IDs.
+
+## Resolution
+
+A coordinator resolves an unresolved visit by submitting a resolution: the visit ID, the set of revision IDs to resolve, and the complete resolved payload. The named set must equal the visit's current head set. If a competing revision has arrived since the coordinator loaded the visit, the resolution is rejected, and the coordinator retries against the new heads. Acceptance is serialized with submissions per visit.
+
+Accepting a resolution creates a resolution revision whose parents are the resolved heads, authored by the coordinator and recorded with the revision IDs it resolved. The visit becomes single-head and resolved. A resolution may carry content from more than one resolved submission, or none from a given head; the record states which submissions were resolved, not which fields each contributed.
+
+A coordinator who wishes only to correct the current record, without resolving competing work, edits it as any worker: base naming the sole head.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Concurrency mechanism | Whole-visit revisions — see [decisions/whole-visit-revisions.md](decisions/whole-visit-revisions.md) | Field-aware three-way merge | The current record must never contain a value no one submitted as a whole visit; merge rules can recombine scientifically related fields without noticing. The team accepts the larger review queue. |
+| Revision ID assignment | Server-assigned | Client-generated revision UUIDs | Tablets need offline-unique identity only for local drafts, which never leave the tablet; the submission envelope already carries an idempotency key, so client revision IDs would add coordination without adding capability. |
+| Resolution shape | Coordinator-authored revision superseding the competing heads, recorded with the resolved revision IDs | Pick-one-head-winner | Coordinators must be able to carry content from more than one submission into the current record; pick-one would force a second step and temporarily drop an accepted edit out of the current state. |
+| While unresolved | No designated current; retrieval returns every head | Designate a provisional current (latest receipt, or the head the caller last fetched) | A provisional current re-introduces arrival order as authority, which the revision model rejects. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+
+1. ✅ Concurrency mechanism: whole-visit revisions — [decisions/whole-visit-revisions.md](decisions/whole-visit-revisions.md).
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Duplicate visits that two tablets create independently under different visit UUIDs for the same plot and date are outside this node's concurrency model: the two submissions never share a visit ID, so no competing-revision work arises. Detection belongs to review work.
+2. A visit that becomes unresolved after it has already been exported does not retract the exported CSV; how a re-export treats it is review-export's decision.
+3. No retention limit is set for superseded revisions; originals are retained for the life of the visit.
+
+## Downstream Obligations
+
+- **tablet-sync** (not yet designed): local drafts record the base revision ID from the state they last fetched; the retry state machine is built around the submission envelope and the acceptance response, which reports whether the edit became a head or joined a competing set; the worker-facing explanation of a competing-revision outcome comes from this node's representation, not from tablet-side inference.
+- **review-export** (not yet designed): the coordinator's comparison work reads head revisions through this node's retrieval; resolution is this node's operation; the export set excludes unresolved visits, keyed on this node's indicator.
